2	
3	Pass a request along a sequence of handlers; each one either handles it, or passes it on, or stops it.
4	
5	## Use it when
6	
7	- several handlers could deal with a request, and which one does depends on the request;
8	- the set of handlers or their order changes per configuration;
9	- new handlers are expected, and today each one would add a branch to a central method.
10	
11	## Do not use it when
12	
13	- there is always exactly one handler: call it;
14	- every handler must always run around the same operation and add something: that is closer to Decorator;
15	- the order and the set never change and fit in one short method.
16	
17	## Shape
18	
19	```java
20	interface ClickHandler {
21	    boolean handle(Click click);           // true when handled; false to let the next one try
22	}
23	
24	final class HandlerChain {
25	    private final List<ClickHandler> handlers;      // the order is decided where the chain is built
26	    HandlerChain(List<ClickHandler> handlers) { this.handlers = List.copyOf(handlers); }
27	
28	    void dispatch(Click click) {
29	        for (ClickHandler handler : handlers) {
30	            if (handler.handle(click)) return;
31	        }
32	    }
33	}
34	
35	final class ButtonHandler implements ClickHandler { /* handles clicks inside the button */ }
36	final class PanelHandler implements ClickHandler { /* handles what the button did not */ }
37	final class WindowHandler implements ClickHandler { /* the last resort */ }
38	```
39	
40	A linked form (each handler holds `next`) is the same pattern.
41	
42	## Done right when
43	
44	- each handler is its own piece and decides only about its own case;
45	- the list (or links) is built in one place, where the configuration is known;
46	- the dispatcher knows no handler by name;
47	- adding a handler means one new class and one change where the chain is built.
48	
